 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Dominated by KDTree construction $O(N \log N)$, k-NN query $O(N k \log N)$, and splatting $O(N_{vis} \cdot r^2)$, where $N_{vis}$ is the number of visible points and $r$ is the splat radius.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N k)$ for storing k-NN indices and $O(N)$ for covariance matrices. Output images are $O(W \cdot H)$.
-- **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-- **Scaling:** Scales with the number of points $N$ and cameras $M$. Performance may degrade with large $k$ or large splat radii.
-- **Strengths:** Dense coverage, no mesh reconstruction required, works directly on raw point clouds.
-- **Limitations:** Computationally intensive for large point clouds, sensitive to $k$ and splat radius parameters.
-- **When to use:** When high-quality, dense point-cloud rendering is needed without mesh reconstruction.
-- **When not to use:** When computational resources are limited or real-time performance is critical.
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \log N)$ for KDTree construction and $O(N \cdot k)$ for neighbour queries. Projection and rasterization are $O(N \cdot r^2)$ where $r$ is the average splat radius.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot k)$ to store neighbour indices and intermediate covariance tensors.
+- **Performance:** Real-time performance is achievable on modern GPUs for point clouds up to millions of points, depending on $k$ and $r$ [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Handles raw point clouds without meshing; adapts splat shape to local geometry, reducing aliasing and gaps [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Sensitive to $k$; too small $k$ leads to noisy covariance, too large $k$ blurs fine details. Performance degrades with very large $k$ or large splat radii [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the context of point-cloud rendering, specifically in the CamPoint repository and related multi-view 2D rendering pipelines [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the EWA splatting technique, adapting it for point clouds by estimating covariance from k-nearest neighbours.
+This method is implemented in the "Point-Splatting-Multi-View-2D-Rendering-Pipeline" repository, which utilizes CamPoint's EWA projection operators [S1, unofficial]. It extends the classic EWA splatting concept by incorporating k-nearest neighbour statistics to estimate local surface orientation and density, enabling more accurate rendering of point-based representations [S1, unofficial].
 
